2Dsquare_pml_extrude
====================

Demonstrates PML added to an *imported* 2D mesh by extrusion, via the optional
pml.input file read by the 2D mesher (mesher2D). It is the general-mesh
counterpart of the "on the fly" PML (mat.dat, used by 2Dsquare_absorbingBC):
here the mesh has no PML, and the mesher grows the PML layers itself.

Note: in 2D the vertical axis is z (stored as the second node coordinate), so
pml.input uses the sides x-, x+, z-, z+. There is no y axis (y-/y+ are rejected).

Pipeline
--------
1. gen_mesh2d.py writes mesh_input.h5: a bare [0,500]x[0,300] domain, 10x6
   Quad4, one solid material, NO PML.
2. mesher2D (menu option 4, HDF5) reads the mesh, reads mater.in as the base
   materials, sees pml.input and extrudes PML element layers on the requested
   sides, deriving the PML materials and writing material.input.
3. sem2d runs the resulting mesh4spec.

Files
-----
  gen_mesh2d.py    mesh generator (bare quad grid, no PML)
  mesh.input       stdin for mesher2D (1 proc, option 4, mesh_input.h5)
  mater.in         base materials (1 solid), format unified with 3D:
                   type Vp Vs Rho Qk Qmu  (no NGLL/Dt)
  pml.input        PML sides + thickness: x-/x+ = 4 layers, z- = 4 layers,
                   z+ left free (free surface)
  input.spec       sem2d config; needs ngll= (NGLL is global, as in 3D) and a
                   pml_infos { pml_type = PML; } section. Ricker pulse at 250,150.
  capteurs.dat     receivers inside the physical domain (2 columns: X Z)
  run.sh           driver: gen mesh -> mesher2D -> (optional) sem2d

Run
---
  MESHER2D=/path/to/build/MESH2D/mesher2D ./run.sh
  # add SEM2D=/path/to/build/SEM2D/sem2d to also run the solver

Expected mesher result
----------------------
Bare grid = 60 quads. After extrusion (4 layers on x-, x+, z-, plus the two
bottom corners W+D and E+D), the physical (non-PML) quad count stays 60 and the
material.input lists the base material plus the derived PML materials (W, E, D,
W+D, E+D). The pulse should be absorbed at the three PML edges while the top
(z+) behaves as a free surface. Compare with 2Dsquare_absorbingBC for the
on-the-fly PML equivalent.
